WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.127 --> 00:00:05.785
OLIVIA: Welcome back to People in the News , Little Fox readers. I hope you like food because

2
00:00:05.786 --> 00:00:11.885
we're going to be talking about cooking today! Isn't that right, Ben? BEN: It sure is, Olivia. Today's person

3
00:00:11.886 --> 00:00:21.267
in the news is Rachael Ray, a very successful and popular American cook, businesswoman, and TV personality. Rachael

4
00:00:21.268 --> 00:00:30.207
Ray was born on August 25, 1968, and grew up near Lake George, New York, a resort town with

5
00:00:30.208 --> 00:00:35.521
lots of hotels and restaurants. Rachael's mom worked in the food industry around Lake George, managing a chain

6
00:00:35.522 --> 00:00:45.254
of restaurants. Since Rachael's mom often brought her kids to work, Rachael spent lots of time in busy kitchens,

7
00:00:45.255 --> 00:00:56.003
both in restaurants and at home, helping her own family prepare meals. In fact, one of Rachael's earliest

8
00:00:56.004 --> 00:01:01.097
memories is of trying to imitate her mother flipping food with a spatula—and then burning her own thumb on

9
00:01:01.098 --> 00:01:10.242
a hot stove! Another important influence on Rachael during her childhood was her grandfather Emmanuel. An emigrant from

10
00:01:10.243 --> 00:01:14.317
the Italian island of Sicily, Emmanuel loved to cook, frequently preparing things like fresh fish and pasta made with

11
00:01:14.318 --> 00:01:19.822
vegetables from his garden. He also introduced Rachael to many different and unusual foods. Later she joked that

12
00:01:19.823 --> 00:01:28.872
her lunchbox used to scare away other kids in the school cafeteria. That was because she packed it with

13
00:01:28.873 --> 00:01:37.713
the strong-smelling foods her grandfather had taught her to love, like sardines, calamari, and garlic! After high school

14
00:01:37.714 --> 00:01:47.231
Rachael worked in several different food stores, learning more about cooking with gourmet ingredients and sharing her knowledge

15
00:01:47.232 --> 00:01:53.778
with the customers. Soon she realized something very important: lots of people wanted to cook with gourmet ingredients or

16
00:01:53.779 --> 00:01:58.966
try new recipes; they simply didn’t have enough time. So in 1996 she started teaching a class called

17
00:01:58.967 --> 00:02:03.375
"30-Minute Meals" to help busy home cooks. The class was so popular that it soon caught the attention of

18
00:02:03.376 --> 00:02:09.358
a local TV station. Before long Rachael was filming short cooking segments on TV. In 1998 she published

19
00:02:09.359 --> 00:02:17.613
her first cookbook of recipes that could be prepared in just thirty minutes. Rachael's big break came in 2001

20
00:02:17.614 --> 00:02:25.613
when she had a chance to cook on a national news program during a huge snowstorm. Because the

21
00:02:25.614 --> 00:02:30.508
bad weather had stranded lots of people at home that day, millions were watching when Rachael prepared several hearty

22
00:02:30.509 --> 00:02:41.249
soups. This successful TV appearance quickly led to Rachael's getting her own cooking show. And within just a

23
00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:48.821
few years, hard-working Rachael transformed herself into a food industry, with a magazine, travel shows, food products, and her

24
00:02:48.822 --> 00:02:58.656
own line of cookware. She even invented a new type of cooking pot—it was oval-shaped, which made it

25
00:02:58.657 --> 00:03:06.490
much easier to cook long sticks of spaghetti! Rachael's cookbooks continued to be bestsellers, and her shows were incredibly

26
00:03:06.491 --> 00:03:14.053
popular, breaking records and winning important awards in the TV industry. By 2006 she was so influential, she

27
00:03:14.054 --> 00:03:15.876
was named by Time magazine as one of the 100 people who were shaping the world. OLIVIA: Wow.

28
00:03:15.877 --> 00:03:26.722
It sounds like Rachael soared to success. But what exactly made her so popular with people? BEN: That's a

29
00:03:26.723 --> 00:03:38.298
good question, Olivia. As Rachael's success grew, lots of people were asking the same thing—especially professionally trained chefs

30
00:03:38.299 --> 00:03:42.288
whose products and shows weren't nearly as popular as Rachael's. Some chefs even began publicly criticizing her, saying that

31
00:03:42.289 --> 00:03:48.802
she had no formal training and had never attended a cooking institute, so she was not a legitimate

32
00:03:48.803 --> 00:03:56.198
authority on food. Rachael's success was largely due to the fact that she was fun to watch. Viewers could

33
00:03:56.199 --> 00:04:01.775
easily identify with her down-to-earth style, and they appreciated all the tips she offered as she cooked. Like

34
00:04:01.776 --> 00:04:12.271
most home cooks, she made mistakes sometimes, accidentally slicing her finger and even setting pans on fire! As for

35
00:04:12.272 --> 00:04:17.174
Rachael herself, she attributed her success to plain hard work and good luck. Still it was obvious to

36
00:04:17.175 --> 00:04:24.004
everyone—even her critics—that Rachael had a special talent for simplifying cooking and spreading her enthusiasm for delicious and healthy

37
00:04:24.005 --> 00:04:30.576
food. That's why, as her success grew, Rachael began turning her attention to kids. She wanted to make

38
00:04:30.577 --> 00:04:39.323
sure that kids ate in a healthy manner, and she wanted them to learn essential cooking skills. So she

39
00:04:39.324 --> 00:04:40.914
began writing cookbooks that were aimed specifically at kids, filling them with kid-friendly recipes. She also included useful

40
00:04:40.915 --> 00:04:51.328
tips, teaching things like how to measure ingredients, handle kitchen equipment, and chop vegetables efficiently. Rachael didn't stop

41
00:04:51.329 --> 00:04:56.802
there. Now that she was a multimillionaire, she was able to fulfill a lifelong dream: she formed her own

42
00:04:56.803 --> 00:04:59.965
charitable foundation devoted to making cooking fun—and healthy—for kids. Called "Yum-o," (a word that Rachael made up for

43
00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:02.715
something that is really yummy or delicious), the foundation's mission is to feed kids living in poverty; provide education

44
00:05:02.716 --> 00:05:07.560
about how to eat healthy foods; and fund scholarships to students who are interested in careers in the

45
00:05:07.561 --> 00:05:16.939
food industry. Through Yum-o's website and outreach programs, kids can share and exchange recipes, play food-related games, and learn

46
00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:23.202
about new and unfamiliar foods. Because of Rachael’s passion for the importance of kids eating well, she eventually

47
00:05:23.203 --> 00:05:32.284
got involved in politics too. She lobbied the U.S. government to contribute more money to free lunches for poor

48
00:05:32.285 --> 00:05:37.035
children and make sure that schools served nutritional, high-quality food to students. OLIVIA: It sounds like Rachael likes

49
00:05:37.036 --> 00:05:44.923
kids as much as she likes food! BEN: She does, Olivia. And she believes strongly that cooking is good

50
00:05:44.924 --> 00:05:49.087
for kids—it's creative, fun, and even teaches math skills! In Rachael's eyes, eating well is a simple way

51
00:05:49.088 --> 00:05:54.006
to enrich your life. OLIVIA: Thanks for that . . . um . . . delicious report on Rachael

52
00:05:54.007 --> 00:06:01.181
Ray, Ben. We'll see you next week, Little Fox readers, when we meet another person in the news!

